{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":31400,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Introduction\n\n**Numerical Analysis** is a branch of mathematics and computer science that develops and studies algorithms for obtaining approximate solutions to mathematical problems that are difficult or impossible to solve exactly. Many real-world problems involve equations, integrals, differential equations, and large systems of calculations that cannot be solved analytically. Numerical analysis provides practical methods to obtain solutions with acceptable accuracy.\n\n## Why Numerical Analysis Is Important\n\nMany scientific and engineering problems involve:\n\n* Weather prediction\n* Aircraft design\n* Machine learning\n* Structural engineering\n* Financial modeling\n* Computer graphics\n* Physics simulations\n\nThese problems often require approximations because exact solutions are either unavailable or too expensive to compute.\n\nFor example:\n\nFind the root of:\n$$\nx^3-x-2=0\n$$\nThis equation has no simple exact algebraic solution, so numerical methods are used to approximate the root.\n\n## Main Areas of Numerical Analysis\n\n### Root Finding\n\nFinding values of (x) that satisfy:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n**Common Methods**\n\n* [Bisection Method](#Bisection-Method)\n* Newton-Raphson Method\n* Secant Method\n* Fixed Point Iteration\n\n**Example**\n\nSolve:\n\n$$\nx^2-4=0\n$$\n\nExact roots:\n\n$$\nx=2,,-2\n$$\n\nUsing numerical methods, we can approximate the roots even if exact solutions are unknown.\n\n### Numerical Linear Algebra\n\nDeals with solving systems of equations:\n\n$$\nAx=b\n$$\n\nExample:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x+y &= 5\\\\\nx-y &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMethods include:\n\n* Gaussian Elimination\n* LU Decomposition\n* Jacobi Method\n* Gauss-Seidel Method\n\nApplications:\n\n* Engineering simulations\n* Machine learning\n* Data science\n\n### Interpolation\n\nInterpolation estimates unknown values between known data points.\n\nExample:\n\n| x | y  |\n| - | -- |\n| 1 | 2  |\n| 2 | 5  |\n| 3 | 10 |\n\nFind the value at (x=2.5).\n\nMethods:\n\n* Linear Interpolation\n* Lagrange Interpolation\n* Newton Interpolation\n* Spline Interpolation\n\n### Numerical Differentiation\n\nApproximates derivatives.\n\nThe derivative formula:\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\approx\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nwhere (h) is very small.\n\nUseful when:\n\n* Function is known only through data\n* Analytical differentiation is difficult\n\n### Numerical Integration\n\nApproximates areas under curves.\n\nExample:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 x^2,dx\n$$\n\nExact answer:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNumerical methods:\n\n* Trapezoidal Rule\n* Simpson's Rule\n* Gaussian Quadrature\n\nApplications:\n\n* Physics\n* Statistics\n* Engineering\n\n### Numerical Solutions of Differential Equations\n\nMany natural phenomena are modeled using differential equations.\n\nExample:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=x+y\n$$\n\nMethods:\n\n* Euler Method\n* Runge-Kutta Methods\n* Finite Difference Methods\n\nApplications:\n\n* Population growth\n* Fluid dynamics\n* Electrical circuits\n\n## Sources of Error\n\nErrors are unavoidable because computers use finite precision.\n\n### Round-off Error\n\nOccurs because computers store only a limited number of digits.\n\nExample:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}=0.333333\\ldots\n$$\n\nComputer stores:\n\n$$\n0.333333\n$$\n\nThe remaining digits are lost.\n\n### Truncation Error\n\nOccurs when an infinite process is replaced by a finite one.\n\nExample:\n\nThe expansion of\n\n$$\ne^x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n1+x+\\frac{x^2}{2!}+\\frac{x^3}{3!}+\\cdots\n$$\n\nUsing only the first few terms introduces truncation error.\n\n### Measurement Error\n\nOccurs when input data itself is inaccurate.\n\nExample:\n\nLength measured as:\n\n$$\n10.2\\pm0.1\\ \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nThe uncertainty affects the final result.\n\n## Characteristics\n\nA good numerical method should have:\n\n1. Accuracy: Produces results close to the true value.\n2. Stability: Small input errors do not cause large output errors.\n3. Efficiency: Requires minimal computation time.\n4. Convergence: Approaches the exact solution as iterations increase.\n\n### Advantages\n\n* Solves problems without exact solutions\n* Handles large datasets\n* Essential for computer simulations\n* Applicable in science and engineering\n* Forms the basis of scientific computing\n\n### Limitations\n\n* Produces approximate, not exact, answers\n* Sensitive to numerical errors\n* Some methods converge slowly\n* Requires computational resources\n\n# Bisection Method\n\nThe **Bisection Method** is one of the simplest and most reliable numerical methods for finding the root of a nonlinear equation.\n\nA **root** of an equation is a value of (x) that makes:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nThe bisection method repeatedly divides an interval into two equal parts and selects the subinterval that contains the root.\n\n## Basic Idea\n\nSuppose we want to solve:\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\nIf we can find two points a and b such that:\n\n$$\nf(a)\\times f(b)<0\n$$\n\nthen f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs.\n\nThis means the graph crosses the x-axis somewhere between a and b, so at least one root exists in that interval (assuming the function is continuous).\n\n## Why It Works\n\nThe method is based on the **Intermediate Value Theorem**.\n\n> If a continuous function changes sign over an interval, then it must cross zero somewhere in that interval.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\nf(1)=-2\n$$\n\n$$\nf(2)=4\n$$\n\nSince the signs are different:\n\n$$\n(-2)(4)=-8<0\n$$\n\na root must exist between 1 and 2.\n\n## Algorithm\n\n### Choose Initial Interval\n\nFind a and b such that:\n\n$$\nf(a)f(b)<0\n$$\n\n### Find Midpoint\n\nUse the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\n### Evaluate Function at Midpoint\n\nCalculate:\n\n$$\nf(m)\n$$\n\n### Select New Interval\n\nThere are three possibilities:\n\n#### Case 1\n\nIf\n\n$$\nf(m)=0\n$$\n\nthen m is the root.\n\n#### Case 2\n\nIf\n\n$$\nf(a)f(m)<0\n$$\n\nthe root lies between a and m.\n\nSet:\n\n$$\nb=m\n$$\n\n#### Case 3\n\nIf\n\n$$\nf(m)f(b)<0\n$$\n\nthe root lies between m and b.\n\nSet:\n\n$$\na=m\n$$\n\n### Repeat\n\nContinue until $|b-a|$ becomes very small, or the desired accuracy is achieved.","metadata":{"_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"%%writefile main.cpp\n\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cmath>\nusing namespace std;\n\n// Example function: f(x) = x^3 - x - 2\ndouble f(double x) {\n    return x * x * x - x - 2;\n}\n\ndouble bisection(double a, double b, double tol = 1e-6, int maxIter = 1000) {\n    if (f(a) * f(b) >= 0) {\n        throw invalid_argument(\"f(a) and f(b) must have opposite signs\");\n    }\n\n    double m;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < maxIter; i++) {\n        m = (a + b) / 2.0;\n\n        if (fabs(f(m)) < tol || (b - a) / 2 < tol)\n            return m;\n\n        if (f(a) * f(m) < 0)\n            b = m;\n        else\n            a = m;\n    }\n\n    return m;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    double root = bisection(1, 2);\n    cout << \"Root = \" << root << endl;\n    return 0;\n}","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-12T03:38:59.664495Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-12T03:38:59.664841Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-12T03:38:59.670612Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-12T03:38:59.664815Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-12T03:38:59.669533Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Overwriting main.cpp\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"code","source":"%%script bash\ng++ main.cpp -o main_exec\n./main_exec","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-12T03:39:01.449538Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-12T03:39:01.449878Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-12T03:39:02.000097Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-12T03:39:01.449849Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-12T03:39:01.999209Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Root = 1.52138\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n## Example Workthrough\n\nFind the root of:\n\n$$\nx^3-x-2=0\n$$\n\n1. Iteration 0\n\nDefine:\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3-x-2\n$$\n\nChoose:\n\n$$\na=1,\\quad b=2\n$$\n\nCalculate:\n\n$$\nf(1)=1-1-2=-2\n$$\n\n$$\nf(2)=8-2-2=4\n$$\n\nSince:\n\n$$\nf(1)f(2)=(-2)(4)=-8<0\n$$\n\na root exists in $[1,2]$.\n\n2. Iteration 1\n\nMidpoint:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1+2}{2}=1.5\n$$\n\nEvaluate:\n\n$$\nf(1.5)=1.5^3-1.5-2\n$$\n\n$$\n=3.375-3.5\n$$\n\n$$\n=-0.125\n$$\n\nTable:\n\n| x   | f(x)   |\n| --- | ------ |\n| 1   | −2     |\n| 1.5 | −0.125 |\n| 2   | 4      |\n\nSince:\n\n$$\nf(1.5)f(2)<0\n$$\n\nroot lies in:\n\n$$\n[1.5,2]\n$$\n\n3. Iteration 2\n\nNew interval:\n\n$$\n[1.5,2]\n$$\n\nMidpoint:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1.5+2}{2}=1.75\n$$\n\nEvaluate:\n\n$$\nf(1.75)=1.75^3-1.75-2\n$$\n\n$$\n=5.359375-3.75\n$$\n\n$$\n=1.609375\n$$\n\nSince:\n\n$$\nf(1.5)f(1.75)<0\n$$\n\nroot lies in:\n\n$$\n[1.5,1.75]\n$$\n\n4. Iteration 3\n\nMidpoint:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1.5+1.75}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nm=1.625\n$$\n\nEvaluate:\n\n$$\nf(1.625)=0.666016\n$$\n\nPositive.\n\nRoot lies in:\n\n$$\n[1.5,1.625]\n$$\n\n5. Iteration 4\n\nMidpoint:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1.5+1.625}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nm=1.5625\n$$\n\nEvaluate:\n\n$$\nf(1.5625)=0.252197\n$$\n\nPositive.\n\nRoot lies in:\n\n$$\n[1.5,1.5625]\n$$\n\n6. Iteration 5\n\nMidpoint:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1.5+1.5625}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nm=1.53125\n$$\n\nEvaluate:\n\n$$\nf(1.53125)=0.059113\n$$\n\nPositive.\n\nRoot lies in:\n\n$$\n[1.5,1.53125]\n$$\n\n7. Iteration 6\n\nMidpoint:\n\n$$\nm=1.515625\n$$\n\nEvaluate:\n\n$$\nf(1.515625)=-0.034054\n$$\n\nNegative.\n\nRoot lies in:\n\n$$\n[1.515625,1.53125]\n$$\n\n**Iteration Summary**\n\n| Iteration | a        | b         | Midpoint   |\n| --------- | -------- | --------- | ---------- |\n| 0         | 1.0000   | 2.0000    | 1.5000     |\n| 1         | 1.5000   | 2.0000    | 1.7500     |\n| 2         | 1.5000   | 1.7500    | 1.6250     |\n| 3         | 1.5000   | 1.6250    | 1.5625     |\n| 4         | 1.5000   | 1.5625    | 1.53125    |\n| 5         | 1.515625 | 1.53125   | 1.5234375  |\n| 6         | 1.515625 | 1.5234375 | 1.51953125 |\n\nContinuing further gives:\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1.52138\n$$\n\n**Error Analysis**\n\nAfter each iteration, the interval length is cut in half.\n\nIf the initial interval length is:\n\n$$\nL_0=b-a\n$$\n\nthen after n iterations:\n\n$$\n\\text{Error}\\leq\\frac{b-a}{2^n}\n$$\n\nThis gives a guaranteed upper bound on the error.\n\nAs, initial interval:\n\n$$\n[1,2]\n$$\n\nLength:\n\n$$\nL_0=1\n$$\n\nAfter 10 iterations:\n\n$$\n\\text{Error}\\le\\frac{1}{2^{10}}\n$$\n\n$$\n=0.0009765625\n$$\n\nThus the root is accurate to about three decimal places.\n\n## Charecterstics\n\n### Advantages\n\n1. Simple: Easy to understand and implement.\n2. Guaranteed Convergence  \n    If:\n    * (f(x)) is continuous\n    * (f(a)) and (f(b)) have opposite signs  \n    the method will always converge to a root.\n3. Stable: Less sensitive to numerical errors than many other methods.\n\n### Disadvantages\n\n1. Slow Convergence\n    * The interval only halves each iteration.\n    * Methods like Newton-Raphson usually converge much faster.\n2. Requires Bracketing: Need initial points satisfying\n\n$$\nf(a)f(b)<0\n$$\n\n3. Cannot Find Some Roots Easily: If the function does not change sign around a root (for example, a repeated root), the method may fail.\n\n\n### Key Points to Remember\n\n* Bisection Method is a **bracketing method**.\n* It works only when the function changes sign in the chosen interval.\n* Each iteration halves the interval containing the root.\n* Convergence is guaranteed for continuous functions with opposite signs at the endpoints.\n* It is slower than methods such as Newton-Raphson, but it is very reliable and widely used as a foundational numerical root-finding technique.","metadata":{}}]}